+# Telephone evidence: official archival sources, attribution limits and missing originals
+
+Research unit opened October 27, 2026; these two acquisitions occurred October 28 UTC (October 27 Eastern). Existing work items: AUD016-A1, TC05/B105 and TC06/B110. This is an addition to the original-record work plan, not a replacement for earlier findings or a completed reconstruction of the calls.
+
+## Result and source scope
+
+Two actual PDFs were acquired unchanged from official National Archives endpoints: the Commission staff report dated August 26, 2004 (T01), and the January 8, 2004 Halleck/Houck interview memorandum (T02, NAID 2609597). They sharpen the source trail and explicitly limit some telephone attributions and reconstructed times. **Neither is the original September 20, 2001 FBI Airphone analysis, a carrier event database, or the missing Department of Justice watch log.** Official hosting identifies the archival representations downloaded; it does not independently authenticate every assertion in them.
+
+The 121-page T01 was extracted for navigation, but only physical pages 1–2, 27–34 and 93–96 were visually inspected: 14 pages. All four T02 pages were visually inspected. Thus 18 of 125 acquired pages were inspected as images; the remaining 107 T01 pages were not. Page numbers below are physical PDF pages and, on the inspected numbered pages, match the printed numbers. Sources, byte counts and SHA-256 values are in [SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json); selected observations and exact page/note references are in [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json). No new native audio decoding or carrier-record replication occurred.
+
+## What the acquired records establish
+
+1. **The staff report expressly limits identification of the Olson calls.** T01 page 94, note 265 says the available records cannot identify which two of four connected calls to unknown numbers were the two Barbara/Ted Olson conversations. Its belief that all four involved the husband's office is attributed to a family canvass, not a displayed unique network identifier. The four starts are 9:15:34, 9:20:15, 9:25:48 and 9:30:56. The third duration is printed as two minutes, 34 seconds: **154 seconds**. This preserves, rather than resolves, the earlier court/carrier **159-second** value and its separate billing/answer/event definitions. See [T01 page 94](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=94).
+
+2. **The report is dependent on the same underlying FBI analysis being sought.** Notes 260, 265 and 271 cite the September 20, 2001 *American Airlines Airphone Usage* report. Those citations do not make three independent corroborations, acquire the missing attachment, or join the Scribd representation to an official release. Page 32's two reported conversations and note 265's four connected records are different units of description; neither is silently substituted for the other.
+
+3. **A printed citation-date inconsistency is real at the image level.** Note 266 on page 94 cites an unnamed witness interview dated **September 14, 2001**. Note 267 on page 95 cites an unnamed witness interview dated **September 4, 2001**, alongside Theodore Olson's September 11 interview. The page image itself shows September 4; this is not merely a text-extraction difference. We have not established whether both citations mean the same witness/report, why the earlier date appears, or which date belongs to the source actually used. This is a low-weight citation/version lead, not proof that an interview occurred before the attacks, that an interview was fabricated, or that call evidence was manufactured. Preserve both entries. [Pages 94–95](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=94).
+
+4. **The note identifies a concrete missing contemporaneous record.** T01 page 95, note 268 names the *Department of Justice Command Center Watch Officer Log, 0001–2400 HRS, September 11, 2001* and reports a 9:33 entry about Ted Olson contacting the center. We acquired that citation, **not the actual log or its entry**, underlying time source, original amendments or custody. The report's page 2 uses Eastern Daylight Time unless otherwise specified; that does not calibrate the unacquired log's clock. The log could independently constrain timing if its actual contents, version and clock can be established. [T01 page 95](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=95).
+
+5. **The interview memorandum explicitly warns against precise recollection-based sequencing.** T02 pages 3–4 record Halleck's difficulty remembering specific times and, in some cases, event order. For Flight 11, the writer derives approximately 7:32 and 7:40 Central from positions 11:27 and 19:39 in the Marquis–Gonzalez recording. Those are staff calculations from another source, not independent clock measurements supplied by the witness. The underlying recording, its start anchor, continuity and clock remain necessary to test them. For Flight 77, the memorandum describes several contacts without assigning exact times. Do not backfill those contacts with the Flight 11 anchors. [T02](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2609597).
+
+6. **The report itself preserves differing timing accounts for a reported Halleck–Herndon contact.** T01 page 94, note 259 contrasts approximately 9:00 (Marquis), 9:05–9:10 (Arpey), and some time after 9:10 (later American Airlines information). It says the Commission found no evidence relating to that call in the Herndon tapes/transcripts it reviewed. Page 31 and note 264 separately describe a 9:16 contact, citing an American transcript and FAA ATCSCC NOM Line 5149, position 34B. These are retrieval pointers. We have not established a unique join between all these accounts and calls, nor independently searched the original recordings. Do not decide that this is one contradictory call, several different calls, a missing recording, or an erroneous clock. [T01 pages 31 and 94](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=31).
+
+7. **The official archive supplies an item-specific route, not the missing content.** The live catalog entry [NAID 2608440](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608440), *FBI knife/explosives evidence from 93 & key 302s: Key 302s*, identifies Team 7 Files, paper container 19. The observed entry says it is not yet available online; no download links were exposed. This title/container match is a candidate route to the FBI report and release chain. It does not prove that identifier M-INT-00158680 / 265A-NY-280350-IN-41864 occurs inside, that an online mirror is complete, or that records were destroyed or improperly withheld. Generic possible-restriction notices are not an item-specific withholding determination.
+
+## Conflicts and contrary evidence retained
+
+- The earlier **154/159-second** values, early connected/later unconnected descriptions, eight calls/seven rows, and direct/collect descriptions remain in their original source-specific contexts. The newly acquired report does not settle their meanings or match call legs.
+- Note 266 reports several failed attempts followed by an accepted operator-assisted collect call, then a direct call. This supports distinguishing attempts, connected legs and conversations; it does not establish that every earlier account uses those same definitions or that a particular caller identity is proved.
+- T02 describes Houck attempting to “cell call” a flight crew through ARINC. The memorandum's wording does not by itself mean a passenger used a cellular telephone. Its technical meaning has not been independently established here.
+- The staff report's own acknowledgement of uncertain identification and differing recollections is contrary to an assumption that every published time and attribution was presented as independently established. Conversely, acknowledgement does not close the missing-source or inconsistent-value questions.
+- Editorial omission, source-version differences, different calls/legs, imperfect recollection and erroneous reconstruction remain possible explanations. Deliberate manipulation is an untested hypothesis; these records do not establish it. What would discriminate are the actual cited reports, recordings, logs, event definitions and version/custody records—not repetition of the staff report.
+
+## Acquisition and process limits
+
+The earlier staff-report URL under `/legislative/research/` returned a 404 in the observed access attempt; the `/files/research/` URL yielded the preserved PDF. This is an access-path observation, not evidence of historical removal. The official court index describes the telephone exhibit as summaries; inspecting that description does not recover raw carrier records.
+
+The initial cap was 16 indexed queries. The unit executed 16 web query strings plus one catalog-within-series query: **17 operations under the conservative counting rule, one beyond the stated cap**. The excess is preserved in the [scope addendum](SCOPE-ADDENDUM.md); no further searches were issued in this unit. Following already observed exact document links and inspecting acquired pages did not constitute a new broad search. The query cap was not retrospectively redefined as met.
+
+Original PDFs, response records, local text extraction and rendered pages remain preserved locally on the verified Intel media store. The public addition contains reviewed notes, source pins and reproducibility records; it does not publish bulk PDFs, unexamined pages, personal telephone numbers, carrier/network identifiers, signatures or private correspondence. Public notes are redundant copies of this local research addition. Earlier reports and source entities remain unchanged; [DIFFS](DIFFS/) records new text and predecessor status. Hash integrity and a source-content check do not authenticate a historical call or establish scientific/historical causation.
+
+## Next original-record work
+
